The pci_hp_register failed with error -16


Possible to investigate and solution to Disable ACPI in VM

indicates a resource conflict where the Standard Hot Plug PCI Controller (shpchp) driver attempts to register a PCI slot that is already registered by another hotplug driver, typically the ACPI hotplug controller or the PCI Express Hot Plug Controller (pciehp).

This error is most commonly observed in Linux virtual machines (especially on Proxmox with the Q35 machine type) or bare-metal systems with conflicting firmware ACPI tables.

Root Causes
Duplicate Registration: Both the hypervisor's virtual hardware (ACPI) and the guest OS's PCI hotplug driver try to claim control of the same virtual PCI slot.
Conflicting Drivers: On physical hardware, the pciehp and shpchp drivers may both attempt to manage the same slot.
Q35 Machine Type: In Proxmox/QEMU, the Q35 chipset defaults to creating more virtual bridges and slots than necessary, triggering these conflicts in the guest OS. 

Solutions
1. Disable ACPI in the VM (Most Effective for Proxmox/QEMU) — If you are using Proxmox or QEMU, disabling ACPI for the guest often resolves the error. 

Go to the VM's Options tab.
Set ACPI to No. 
Note: Ensure qemu-guest-agent is installed in the guest so it can still receive shutdown commands. 
